.Dd August 2, 2026
.Dt GITHUB-TOKEN-BROKER 8
.Os
.\" -------------------------------------------------------------------------
.Sh NAME
.Nm github-token-broker
.Nd mint short-lived, repository-scoped GitHub App installation tokens
.\" -------------------------------------------------------------------------
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Fl -config Ar path
.\" -------------------------------------------------------------------------
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a local service that mints GitHub App installation tokens on demand.
Each token is restricted to one repository, expires within the hour, and is
attributed to the App rather than to a person.
.Pp
It replaces the personal access token that automation otherwise ends up
holding: long-lived, broadly scoped, and attributed to a human.
.Pp
Only this process reads the App private key, signs a JWT, or calls GitHub.
.Pp
That separates duties.
It is
.Em not
an isolation boundary: anything that can reach the endpoint can obtain a token
for any allowlisted repository.
Restricting who can reach it is the deployment's job, and depends on which
transport is configured.
.Pp
The only argument is the configuration path, which must be absolute:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -config Ar path
Absolute path to the JSON configuration document described in
.Xr github-token-broker-config 5 .
.El
.Pp
There is no
.Fl -version
or
.Fl -help ;
either exits 64 with the usage line.
.Pp
The process runs in the foreground.
It shuts down gracefully on
.Dv SIGINT
and
.Dv SIGTERM .
Changing configuration requires a restart.
.\" -------------------------------------------------------------------------
.Sh DEPLOYMENT
The broker runs on the host, not in a container.
It holds the App private key, and a container's image and memory are readable
by anyone who can run the runtime, which puts the key within reach of every
workload on that daemon.
.Pp
Clients in containers reach it by bind-mounting the socket; see
.Xr github-token 1 .
.Pp
Run it as a dedicated account that does nothing else.
That account owns the private key, mode
.Ql 0400 ,
and the socket directory.
.\" -------------------------------------------------------------------------
.Sh LOGGING
Two streams, split by whether the broker went on to serve.
.Pp
Standard error carries the one fatal startup failure and nothing else:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
private key error: The private key could not be read from
"/etc/github-token-broker/app.pem".
  Could not find a part of the path
  '/etc/github-token-broker/app.pem'.
.Ed
.Pp
Standard output carries every log line thereafter:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
info: github-token-broker[3000]
      Broker ready for app 123456 installation 789012
      with 2 allowlisted repositories
.Ed
.Pp
A service manager wanting one record captures both.
.Pp
The level is
.Ql Information ,
raised or lowered through
.Ev Logging__LogLevel__Default ,
which takes
.Ql Trace ,
.Ql Debug ,
.Ql Information ,
.Ql Warning ,
.Ql Error ,
or
.Ql Critical .
.Pp
No log line ever contains a token, a JWT, or key material.
.\" -------------------------------------------------------------------------
.Sh API
Requests are HTTP over whichever transports
.Ic listen
configures.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic GET /health
Liveness, and the number of repositories in the allowlist.
Never mints.
.It Ic POST /v1/token
Returns a cached or freshly minted token.
.It Ic POST /v1/check
Confirms authorization without minting, returning the repository and its
canonical permission ceiling.
.El
.Pp
Both
.Ic POST
routes take:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{"host": "github.com", "repository": "OWNER/REPOSITORY"}
.Ed
.Pp
.Ic /v1/token
answers:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{"token": "...", "expires_at": "2026-08-02T12:00:00Z"}
.Ed
.Pp
.Ic /health
answers:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{"status": "ok", "repositories": 2}
.Ed
.Pp
Requests are bounded: only the declared method per route, only
.Ql application/json ,
at most 4096 bytes, and a JSON depth of 8.
.Pp
Every refusal is one status and one message, so error text cannot be used to
probe the allowlist.
The precise reason goes to the log instead.
.Pp
No route asks for a credential.
Reaching the socket is the authorization, and a caller that can write to it
could mint for the whole allowlist by any route.
.\" -------------------------------------------------------------------------
.Sh TRANSPORTS
Which transport to configure, and what each costs, is in
.Xr github-token-broker-config 5 .
What follows is what the broker does with them at startup.
.\"
.Ss The socket directory
Traversal of the directory holding the socket is the outer half of the socket's
authorization.
.Pp
When the directory does not exist, the broker creates it
.Ql 0700 .
When it already exists and is writable beyond its owner, the broker refuses to
start
.Em unless
the sticky bit is set.
Write permission on the directory is the right to unlink the socket and bind
another in its place, so a narrow mode on the socket alone would not be enough.
The sticky bit is what makes a shared directory such as
.Pa /tmp
safe: everyone may create, only the owner may unlink.
Traversal for a group stays available through
.Ql 0750 .
.Pp
Only the mode is checked, never the owner.
A directory another account owns at
.Ql 0755
passes, and that account can unlink the socket and bind its own in its place.
Give the socket a directory the broker's own account owns; under systemd that is
.Ql RuntimeDirectory= .
.\"
.Ss The socket mode
Binding creates the socket file, and the broker chmods it a moment later, so in
between its mode comes from the umask.
The broker narrows the umask across that moment, so the socket is created
owner-only and every widening after it is one the configuration asked for.
An inherited
.Ql 002
would otherwise leave it group-writable, which on a socket is the right to
connect.
.Pp
The mode is applied by the broker rather than left to the process umask.
Connecting needs write permission on the socket file, so a umask would decide
who may mint.
.\"
.Ss \&An occupied path
Binding fails if the socket path is occupied, and nothing is removed to free it.
A socket left by an unclean shutdown reads the same as a mistyped path naming a
real file, and removing the wrong one destroys whatever it named.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ github-token-broker --config /etc/github-token-broker/config.json
configuration error: Another process is already listening on
"/run/github-token-broker/broker.sock".
.Ed
.Pp
A path that is a directory or a symbolic link is refused outright.
Clearing an orphan belongs to the operator or the service manager; systemd's
.Ql RuntimeDirectory=
recreates the directory empty on every start.
.\" -------------------------------------------------------------------------
.Sh TOKEN MINTING
An RS256 JWT is signed with
.Ql iat
backdated 60 seconds, so a host clock running slightly ahead of GitHub's does
not have its tokens rejected as issued in the future.
It stays in memory and is never returned through the API.
.Pp
The broker then calls
.Ql POST /app/installations/{id}/access_tokens
with exactly one repository name and exactly the configured permission map.
The repository is sent without its owner, since the installation implies it.
.Pp
A response is refused unless it proves the narrowing held:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
a usable token,
.It
an expiry in the future,
.It
a
.Ql repository_selection
of
.Ql selected ,
.It
exactly one repository, matching the request,
.It
no permission beyond the configured ceiling, allowing for the standing
.Ql metadata: read
noted in
.Xr github-token-broker-config 5 .
.El
.Pp
Token values are treated as opaque: no prefix, format, or length is assumed.
.\"
.Ss Key rotation
Generate a new key in the App settings, replace the file, confirm a mint
succeeds, then delete the old key at GitHub.
No restart is needed, since the key is re-read on every mint.
.\" -------------------------------------------------------------------------
.Sh CACHE
In memory only, keyed by normalized repository
.Em and
effective permissions, so narrowing a ceiling cannot silently reuse a token
minted under a broader one.
.Pp
A token is reused only while more than
.Ic token_refresh_margin_seconds
remains before expiry.
There is no refresh loop: a token is minted by the first request that finds none
fresh enough.
.Pp
Concurrent misses for one key are coalesced onto a single mint.
A failed mint clears its entry, so it fails only the callers already waiting on
it and never poisons a later request.
.\" -------------------------------------------------------------------------
.Sh EXIT STATUS
Following the conventional
.In sysexits.h
values, so a service manager can distinguish an operator mistake from a runtime
fault.
.Bl -tag -width "64" -compact
.It Sy 0
Shut down normally.
.It Sy 64
The command line was wrong.
.It Sy 66
The private key could not be read or parsed.
.It Sy 70
Startup failed for a reason the broker does not classify.
The message names what threw; the other statuses are the ones an operator acts
on directly.
.It Sy 71
The socket was bound but its mode could not be applied.
.It Sy 78
Configuration was missing or invalid, including a listener that could not bind.
.El
.Pp
All of these happen before the broker serves a request, so restarting on
failure retries a condition that has not changed.
The key has its own status because it is the one fixed somewhere other than the
configuration file.
.\" -------------------------------------------------------------------------
.Sh EXAMPLES
Run against a configuration file:
.Dl $ github-token-broker --config /etc/github-token-broker/config.json
.Pp
Confirm it is serving, over a Unix socket.
The host in the URL is never resolved, since the socket determines the peer:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ curl --unix-socket /run/github-token-broker/broker.sock \e
    http://localhost/health
{"status":"ok","repositories":2}
.Ed
.\" -------------------------------------------------------------------------
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
Mint failures are classified, because the class decides what happens next and
what an operator should check.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic AppUnauthorized
GitHub answered
.Sy 401 :
wrong key, clock skew, or a rotated key.
The whole cache is dropped, since every cached grant was minted with the
credential GitHub just rejected.
The client sees
.Sy 409 ,
since only clock skew clears on its own and a client retrying a revoked key
never stops.
.It Ic InstallationForbidden
GitHub answered
.Sy 403 ;
the installation is suspended.
The client sees
.Sy 409 .
.It Ic InstallationOrRepositoryMissing
GitHub answered
.Sy 404 ;
the installation or the repository is gone.
The client sees
.Sy 409 .
.It Ic PermissionDrift
GitHub answered
.Sy 422 ;
configuration asks for more than the App grants.
The client sees
.Sy 500 .
.It Ic UntrustworthyResponse
The response could not be proven narrow.
The client sees
.Sy 500 .
.It Ic PrivateKeyUnusable
The key was readable at startup and is not now, which is a rotation caught
mid-flight.
The client sees
.Sy 500 .
.It Ic UnrecognizedStatus
GitHub, or whatever answered in its place, returned a status this broker has no
reading for.
Nothing about it says a later attempt fares better, so the client sees
.Sy 500 .
.It Ic Unavailable
Transport failure, or a
.Sy 500 ,
.Sy 502 ,
.Sy 503 ,
or
.Sy 504
GitHub may recover from.
These all fail in milliseconds, so the mint is attempted again inside the same
request for as long as the client's wait affords.
The client sees
.Sy 503
once those attempts run out.
.It Ic TimedOut
GitHub did not answer within 15 seconds.
The client sees
.Sy 503 .
.It Ic RateLimited
GitHub answered
.Sy 429 ,
or
.Sy 403
carrying a rate-limit header.
The client sees
.Sy 503 .
.El
.Pp
.Ic Unavailable ,
.Ic TimedOut ,
and
.Ic RateLimited
answer
.Sy 503 ,
being the classes that clear without anyone acting and are retried on the next
request.
Every other class tells a client to go and look.
.Pp
A failed mint always drops whatever that grant had cached, since a token is only
minted once the cached one is no longer fresh enough to serve.
.\"
.Ss Reading back from a client exit status
.Xr github-token 1
reports a status rather than the HTTP one.
To reach the classes above from what a script saw:
.Bl -tag -width "78" -compact
.It Sy 69
The broker was never reached, so nothing here applies.
Check that it is running and that the endpoint matches what it binds.
.It Sy 70
.Sy 500 :
.Ic PermissionDrift ,
.Ic UntrustworthyResponse ,
.Ic PrivateKeyUnusable ,
or
.Ic UnrecognizedStatus .
.It Sy 75
.Sy 503 :
.Ic Unavailable ,
.Ic TimedOut ,
or
.Ic RateLimited .
.It Sy 77
Refused before any mint was attempted, so no class was reached.
The repository is absent from the allowlist.
.It Sy 78
.Sy 409 :
.Ic AppUnauthorized ,
.Ic InstallationForbidden ,
or
.Ic InstallationOrRepositoryMissing .
.El
.Pp
Only the log distinguishes the classes sharing a status.
