.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
Register a private App owned by the account that owns the target repositories.
Leave webhooks inactive, request no user authorization, and install it on
.Em only select repositories .
.Pp
Grant no more than the work requires.
.Ql contents: write ,
.Ql pull_requests: write ,
and read on
.Ql actions ,
.Ql checks ,
and
.Ql statuses
covers branch pushes and pull requests.
Leave workflow, administration, secrets, and environment permissions off.
.Pp
Naming
.Ql metadata: read
is optional, and worth doing so the ceiling matches what a token carries.
The names and levels this map accepts are in
.Xr github-token-broker-config 5 .
.Pp
Protect each default branch with a ruleset that requires pull requests, blocks
force pushes and deletion, and does
.Em not
list the App as a bypass.
App permissions govern what the API allows; branch rules govern what may reach a
protected branch.
Both layers are needed.
.Pp
An App installed on one account grants no access to another's repositories, even
when the App owner is a collaborator there.
Forking each target repository into the account that owns the App is the setup
this design assumes.
Creating a pull request requires
.Ql pull_requests: write
on the
.Em base
repository, so a token scoped to a fork cannot open one against an upstream.
